# Copy to ~/.config/kmsrdp/kmsrdp.env (mode 600 - it holds a password) and
# edit, then: systemctl --user enable --now kmsrdp.service
#
# If left unset, kmsrdp-server writes a random one-shot password to a 0600
# file under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kmsrdp/ (printed to the journal only as a path,
# or in full if stderr is a TTY).

KMSRDP_USER=kmsrdp
# Prefer a file over putting the password in this env (it is visible in
# /proc/<pid>/environ). systemd: LoadCredential=kmsrdp.password:/path
# KMSRDP_PASSWORD_FILE=/etc/kmsrdp/rdp-password
KMSRDP_PASSWORD=change-me

# Listen address and port. Defaults are 127.0.0.1 and 3389 (not the public
# Internet). Use 0.0.0.0 only on a trusted LAN/VPN.
# KMSRDP_BIND=0.0.0.0
# KMSRDP_PORT=3389

# NLA (CredSSP/NTLMv2) is required by default. Set to 0 only if a client
# cannot do NLA.
# KMSRDP_REQUIRE_NLA=0

# Maximum authenticated sessions (default 1, max 32). Extra clients are
# dropped. Sessions share one desktop, one uinput device, and one FUSE
# drive mount per DosName.
# KMSRDP_MAX_SESSIONS=1

# Optional: capture one specific active DRM connector. Use CARD:CONNECTOR
# (for example card1:DP-1) if the connector name is ambiguous across cards.
# KMSRDP_DISPLAY=DP-1

# Optional: TLS identity. By default a self-signed cert/key is created once
# under $XDG_STATE_HOME/kmsrdp (systemd StateDirectory) and reused.
# KMSRDP_TLS_HOSTS=myhost,192.168.1.10
# KMSRDP_TLS_DIR=/path/to/dir
# KMSRDP_TLS_CERT=/path/to/tls.crt
# KMSRDP_TLS_KEY=/path/to/tls.key
# KMSRDP_TLS_EPHEMERAL=1

# Optional: structured logging (stderr). Same filter syntax as RUST_LOG.
# KMSRDP_LOG=info
# KMSRDP_LOG=kmsrdp=debug,rdpcore_server=info
# KMSRDP_LOG_FORMAT=json

# Startup checks refuse privileged ports (<1024) without CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE,
# empty KMSRDP_PASSWORD when set, and invalid KMSRDP_DISPLAY / TLS path pairs.
# Missing fusermount3 is a warning only.
